---
import Callout from "../../../guide/Callout.astro";
import { sdk, sdkTree } from "../../../guide/extensions/sdk.ts";
import GuidePage from "../../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Member from "../../../guide/reference/Member.astro";
import Members from "../../../guide/reference/Members.astro";
import Path from "../../../guide/Path.astro";
import Task from "../../../guide/Task.astro";
import { leavesSite, REPOSITORY, sitePath } from "../../../site/links.ts";
---

<GuidePage
  slug="extensions"
  lead="These pages show how to write one with the Katari SDK and offer it in an extension store of your own."
>
  <Task id="what-an-extension-is" title="What an extension is">
    <p>
      An extension is an Android app with no screens. Katari finds it among the installed apps, loads its code, and asks it for
      <strong>sources</strong>. A source is one site, usually in one language, and Katari calls these methods on it:
    </p>
    <Members>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getPopularContent(page: Int): EntryPageResult<SEntry>">
        For the source's <strong>Popular</strong> list, one page at a time.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getLatestUpdates(page: Int): EntryPageResult<SEntry>">
        For the source's <strong>Latest</strong> list, one page at a time.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getSearchContent(page: Int, query: String, filters: EntryFilterList): EntryPageResult<SEntry>">
        For a search in the source, with the user's <a href={sitePath("guide/extensions/filters/")}>filters</a>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="suspend fun refresh(entry: SEntry, existingChapters: List<SEntryChapter>, fetchDetails: Boolean, fetchChapters: Boolean): EntryRefresh">
        When a title's page opens or is refreshed, for its details, its chapters or both, and when library updates look for new
        chapters.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getMedia(chapter: SEntryChapter, selection: PlaybackSelection = PlaybackSelection()): EntryMedia">
        When a chapter or episode opens or is downloaded.
      </Member>
    </Members>
    <p>
      <a href={sitePath("guide/extensions/source/")}>Write a source</a> covers each of them. One source can serve comics, video and
      books, even mixed in one list, since every title it returns says which kind it is.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="sdk" title="The SDK">
    <p>
      Extensions are written in Kotlin against the Katari SDK. Latest published version is {sdk.version}. It is published on JitPack from the
      <a href={sdkTree("")} {...leavesSite()}><code>{sdk.tag}</code> tag</a> of Katari's repository:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={sdkTree("entry-source-api")} {...leavesSite()}><code>entry-source-api</code></a> holds the source interfaces and models.</li>
      <li><a href={sdkTree("book-api")} {...leavesSite()}><code>book-api</code></a> describes books and their files.</li>
      <li>
        <a href={sdkTree("core/common")} {...leavesSite()}><code>core-common</code></a> holds the network client and what a source sends
        requests with, as <a href={sitePath("guide/extensions/source/#network")}>Requests</a> lists.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/SDK-CHANGELOG.md`} {...leavesSite()}>SDK changelog</a> lists what each release added, and every interface
      and field is also documented where the SDK declares it.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="versions" title="Which Katari loads it">
    <p>
      An extension's <code>versionName</code> is the SDK's major and minor version, a dot, and the extension's own release number:
      <code>{sdk.family}.4</code> is the fourth release of an extension built with SDK {sdk.family}. Unless the manifest names the SDK in
      <code>tachiyomix.extensionLib</code>, Katari reads everything before the last dot as the SDK, so a <code>versionName</code> of <code>{sdk.family}</code> alone reads as SDK {sdk.family.split(".")[0]} and
      isn't loaded.
    </p>
    <p>
      Katari loads extensions built with SDK 3.0 up to the newest it knows, whatever their patch version. SDK 3.0 changed the source
      interfaces, so an extension built with SDK 2.x is listed under <strong>Not loaded</strong>, and its store doesn't offer it, until
      it is rebuilt. The same goes for one built with a newer SDK, until the user updates Katari.
    </p>
    <p>
      Katari also loads extensions made for Mihon and Tachiyomi, built with extensions-lib 1.6, and anime extensions made for Aniyomi,
      built with its extensions-lib 14, 16 or 17. New extensions are written against the Katari SDK.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="trust" title="What users see before it runs">
    <p>
      An extension runs inside Katari with all of its access, so Katari checks every one before loading it. It is trusted straight away
      when one of the keys its APK is signed with is the key of an extension store the user added. Otherwise the user has to trust it,
      and Katari remembers that for this package, version code and key only, so each update asks again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path>, an extension that fails a check is listed under <strong>Not loaded</strong>. Katari checks
      in this order and stops at the first that fails:
    </p>
    <table class="terms">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Reason</th> <th>Cause</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Missing information</td>
          <td>The APK has no meta-data or no <code>versionName</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Unsupported SDK</td>
          <td>
            The SDK, read from <code>tachiyomix.extensionLib</code> or else from the <code>versionName</code>, isn't one this Katari loads,
            as <a href="#versions">above</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td>Unsigned</td> <td>The APK has no signature.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Untrusted</strong></td>
          <td>No store's key signs it and the user hasn't trusted this version. The user taps the shield and <strong>Trust</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Filtered</strong></td>
          <td>
            Its content warning isn't among <strong>Allowed content warnings</strong> under <Path>Settings › Browse</Path>. All are
            allowed at first, and installed extensions are filtered only while <strong>Also apply to installed extensions</strong> is on.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td>Missing information</td> <td>The manifest has no <code>tachiyomi.extension.class</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Failed</td>
          <td>
            Creating one of its classes or sources threw. The extension's info button shows the error and its stack trace, which also go
            to <code>adb logcat</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Conflict</strong></td>
          <td>
            Other installed extensions already provide every one of its sources, as <a href={sitePath("guide/extensions/source/#base-class")}>a
            source's <code>id</code></a> describes.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      An APK Android can't read at all, such as an incomplete download installed as <strong>Private</strong>, is listed under
      <strong>Not loaded</strong> too, and the user is told to install it again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only <strong>Untrusted</strong>, <strong>Filtered</strong> and <strong>Conflict</strong> are labelled in the list; the info button
      explains the others.
      <a href={sitePath("guide/extensions/store/")}>Publish an extension store</a> shows how to sign extensions so that users of your store
      never see <strong>Untrusted</strong>.
    </p>
    <Callout label="No secrets in code">
      <p>An APK can be unpacked by anyone who installs it. Don't put API keys or passwords in an extension; let users enter their own in its settings.</p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
